(Title) Tj
T*
(Soft Robotic Exoskeleton Training Enhances Gait Parameters in Parkinson Disease: Results) Tj
T*
(of a Pilot Randomized Study) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Robotic rehabilitation tools may provide advantages over conventional therapy by) Tj
T*
(delivering precisely-calibrated motor assistance. Fifty-six individuals with Parkinson) Tj
T*
(disease underwent randomization to standard rehabilitation versus standard rehabilitation) Tj
T*
(plus four weeks of soft exoskeleton training \(five times weekly, 20 minutes per session\).) Tj
T*
(The exoskeleton-augmented group demonstrated significantly greater improvements in stride) Tj
T*
(length symmetry and walking speed compared to controls. Specifically, stride length) Tj
T*
(increased by approximately 0.15 meters on both sides, and ankle plantarflexion flexibility) Tj
T*
(improved substantially. Measures of motor severity on standardized clinical scales) Tj
T*
(decreased more in the exoskeleton group. Additionally, participants receiving robotic) Tj
T*
(assistance showed improved balance-related subscale scores and modest gains in cognitive) Tj
T*
(screening measures. Improvements in ankle mobility showed strong positive correlation with) Tj
T*
(overall gait velocity gains, suggesting a mechanistic link. No serious adverse events) Tj
T*
(occurred. Soft robotic exoskeleton training represents a feasible and effective adjunct to) Tj
T*
(conventional rehabilitation for reducing gait dysfunction in Parkinson disease.) Tj
ET
